 Reflection on Prompt Design and Image Outcomes
 Overview

This reflection explains how the two prompts from Phase 2 were developed, how they guided the resulting images, and what artistic or conceptual choices shaped each design. It also evaluates the effectiveness of each prompt and identifies areas for improvement as I continue learning how to write clear, descriptive visual prompts.
Prompt A Reflection — Charcoal Candle

The goal of Prompt A was to create an intimate charcoal-style drawing of a single candle, using dramatic contrast and textured shading to emphasize mood and atmosphere. I focused on describing the candle as the central subject, the soft glow of the flame, and the expressive charcoal marks that define the background. These details were meant to guide the image toward a handcrafted, quiet, and contemplative aesthetic.

The resulting image successfully captured the tonal range and grainy texture associated with charcoal. The flame became a natural focal point, and the surrounding shadows helped reinforce the sense of stillness I wanted. If I revise this prompt in the future, I would include more detail about the environment around the candle—such as whether it sits on a table, in a dark room, or within a symbolic setting—to give the composition additional narrative depth.

---

Prompt B Reflection — Linocut Flower

Prompt B aimed to produce a stylized flower in the bold, graphic style of a linocut print. I emphasized thick carved outlines, high contrast between black and white, and the textured grooves that mimic traditional carving tools. The intention was to create a floral image that felt both organic and intentionally sculpted.

The final image reflected these qualities well. The strong outlines and simplified shapes matched the linocut aesthetic, and the negative space helped the flower stand out clearly. One improvement I might make is specifying the type of flower or adding more detail about the background pattern, which could help create a more distinctive or symbolic composition.

---

Overall Reflection

Writing these prompts helped me understand how descriptive language directly shapes visual outcomes. When the prompt clearly identifies the subject, style, and artistic intention, the resulting image aligns more closely with the original concept. I also learned that prompts need full sentences and concrete details—not tool metadata—to meet assignment expectations and communicate effectively.

Going forward, I plan to include more environmental context, emotional tone, and specific visual cues in my prompts. This will help me create images that feel more intentional and narratively rich.


